  "decline_reason": "I cannot predict how your particular application will be decided, but can explain the examination practice and statutory rules in the supplied passages.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s practice, a section 44 objection should be raised if the Registrar or delegate is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the provisions apply; doubt about whether a ground exists should be resolved against raising it.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part20/5/5/6/2",
          "quote": "Given the presumption of registrability, a critical approach must be taken in applying these tests so that only soundly based citations are raised."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part20/5/5/6/2",
          "quote": "However, it the Registrar or their delegate is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the provisions of section 44 are relevant to the application then the ground for rejection should be raised. If there is a doubt that a ground for rejection exists, then the Registrar or their delegate is not satisfied and the ground for rejection should not be raised."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Once satisfied that a ground exists, the Registrar or delegate must, under the Manual’s practice, state and document it specifically and with support, and the presumption of registrability no longer applies.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part20/4/4/2",
          "quote": "If the Registrar or their delegate is satisfied that a ground (or grounds) for rejection does exist, then the presumption of registrability no longer applies. The ground or grounds upon which rejection is based must be articulated in a specific and supported manner, and documented in line with administrative requirements."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For goods, section 44(1) requires rejection, subject to subsections (3) and (4), where the specified similarity conditions are met and the applicant’s priority date is not earlier than the other mark’s priority date.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(1) Subject to subsections (3) and (4), an application for the registration of a trade mark (applicant’s trade mark) in respect of goods (applicant’s goods) must be rejected if: (a) the applicant’s trade mark is substantially identical with, or deceptively similar to: (i) a trade mark registered by another person in respect of similar goods or closely related services; or (ii) a trade mark whose registration in respect of similar goods or closely related services is being sought by another person; and (b) the priority date for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark in respect of the applicant’s goods is not earlier than the priority date for the registration of the other trade mark in respect of the similar goods or closely related services."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "For services, section 44(2) imposes the corresponding rejection requirement for substantially identical or deceptively similar marks covering similar services or closely related goods, again subject to subsections (3) and (4) and the priority-date condition.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(2) Subject to subsections (3) and (4), an application for the registration of a trade mark (applicant’s trade mark) in respect of services (applicant’s services) must be rejected if: (a) it is substantially identical with, or deceptively similar to: (i) a trade mark registered by another person in respect of similar services or closely related goods; or (ii) a trade mark whose registration in respect of similar services or closely related goods is being sought by another person; and (b) the priority date for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark in respect of the applicant’s services) is not earlier than the priority date for the registration of the other trade mark in respect of the similar services or closely related goods."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "An earlier-mark objection does not necessarily prevent acceptance: under section 44(3), the Registrar may accept where satisfied of honest concurrent use or other circumstances making acceptance proper, subject to any conditions or limitations considered appropriate.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(3) If the Registrar in either case is satisfied: (a) that there has been honest concurrent use of the 2 trade marks; or (b) that, because of other circumstances, it is proper to do so; the Registrar may accept the application for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark subject to any conditions or limitations that the Registrar thinks fit to impose."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under section 44(4), the Registrar may not reject because of the other mark if satisfied that the applicant, alone or with its predecessor in title, continuously used its mark from before the other mark’s relevant priority date until the applicant’s priority date.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(4) If the Registrar in either case is satisfied that the applicant, or the applicant and the predecessor in title of the applicant, have continuously used the applicant’s trade mark for a period: (a) beginning before the priority date for the registration of the other trade mark in respect of: (i) the similar goods or closely related services; or (ii) the similar services or closely related goods; and (b) ending on the priority date for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark; the Registrar may not reject the application because of the existence of the other trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual describes a basic acceptance period of 15 months from the first report, or from a further report raising new grounds, and states that an application lapses if not accepted within the prescribed period or that period as extended.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part16/1/1/1",
          "quote": "The legislation states that \"an application lapses if it is not accepted within the prescribed period or within that period as extended in accordance with the regulations\" (section 37). The prescribed period is 15 months from the date of the first report, or 15 months from the date of any further report that raises new grounds for rejection of the application."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "According to the Manual, requesting an ex parte hearing on an examination matter triggers deferment of acceptance without a separate extension or deferment request, until the hearing request is withdrawn or the Registrar decides the examination, whichever occurs first.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part16/1/1/1",
          "quote": "If an applicant requests an ex parte hearing on an exam matter, the Registrar will defer acceptance of that application. The applicant need not separately apply for an extension of time or deferment. This deferment will end on the earlier of: the day the applicant withdraws the request to be heard, or the day the Registrar decides the outcome of the examination."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
